// Practice quiz: Strings and string methods
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/strings-and-string-methods

::o1 concatenation order::What is the value of 1 + 2 + " apples"? {
=The String "3 apples"#Correct. + works left to right; 1 + 2 is the int 3, and only then is the String joined.
~The String "12 apples"#Not quite. That happens when the String comes first, as in "apples " + 1 + 2.
~A compile error#Not quite. Joining a number and a String with + is allowed.
~The int 3#Not quite. Once a String joins in, the result is a String.
}

::o1 text block::What does a text block, written between two sets of three double quotes, let you write easily? {
=A String that spans several lines#Correct. Text blocks hold multi-line text without writing a line-break escape and a + on every line.
~A comment the compiler ignores#Not quite. A text block is a String value, not a comment.
~A single char#Not quite. A char uses single quotes and holds one character.
~A String that can be changed later#Not quite. A text block produces an ordinary, immutable String.
}

::o2 last index::String w \= "planet"; Which call returns the last character? {
=w.charAt(w.length() - 1)#Correct. Indexes start at 0, so the last one is length() - 1, here 5.
~w.charAt(w.length())#Not quite. Index 6 is past the end and throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException.
~w.charAt(6)#Not quite. "planet" has 6 characters at indexes 0 to 5.
~w.substring(6)#Not quite. substring(6) returns the empty String, not the last character.
}

::o2 substring end::String w \= "planet"; What does w.substring(1, 4) return? {
="lan"#Correct. It starts at index 1 and stops before index 4.
~"lane"#Not quite. The end index 4 is not included.
~"plan"#Not quite. Index 1 is the second character, l.
~"anet"#Not quite. That would be substring(2).
}

::o2 indexOf missing::What does "banana".indexOf("x") return? {
=-1#Correct. indexOf returns -1 when the text does not occur.
~0#Not quite. 0 is the index of the first character, b.
~null#Not quite. indexOf returns an int, never null.
~It throws an exception#Not quite. A missing match is reported with -1, not an exception.
}

::o3 immutability::String city \= "oslo"; city.toUpperCase(); What does city hold now? {
="oslo"#Correct. toUpperCase returns a new value; without city \= city.toUpperCase(); the variable is unchanged.
~"OSLO"#Not quite. That is the returned value, which this line throws away.
~"Oslo"#Not quite. Nothing capitalizes only the first letter here.
~null#Not quite. city still refers to its original String.
}
